.TH GRIDSNAP 1 "2026-11-22" "gridsnap 0.2.0" "User commands"
.SH NAME
gridsnap, gridsnap\-tiler, gridsnap\-daemon, gridsnap\-editor, gridsnap\-picker,
gridsnap\-hotcorners, gridsnap\-ctl, setup\-hotkeys \- window tiling into zones
for Cinnamon/X11
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBgridsnap\-tiler\fP [\fB\-\-monitor\fP \fIN\fP] [\fB\-\-conf\fP \fIPATH\fP] {\fBlist\fP |
\fBapply\fP \fIZONE\fP | \fBmove\fP \fIDIRECTION\fP | \fBkeys\fP | \fBarrange\fP | \fBstack\fP
\fIZONE\fP | \fBtabs\-next\fP | \fBundo\fP | \fBprofile\fP} [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fP] [\fB\-\-verify\fP]
[\fB\-\-quiet\fP] ...
.br
\fBgridsnap\-daemon\fP [\fB\-\-conf\fP \fIPATH\fP] [\fB\-\-verbose\fP] [\fB\-\-once\fP]
[\fB\-\-socket\fP \fIPATH\fP | \fB\-\-no\-socket\fP]
.br
\fBgridsnap\-editor\fP [\fB\-\-conf\fP \fIPATH\fP] [\fB\-\-monitor\fP \fIN\fP] [\fB\-\-shot\fP
\fIFILE.png\fP]
.br
\fBgridsnap\-picker\fP [\fB\-\-conf\fP \fIPATH\fP] [\fB\-\-monitor\fP \fIN\fP] [\fB\-\-window\fP
\fIXID\fP] [\fB\-\-select\fP \fIZONE\fP] [\fB\-\-workspace\fP \fIN\fP|current|conf] [\fB\-\-hud\fP
[\fB\-\-at\fP \fIX,Y\fP | \fB\-\-follow\fP]] [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fP] [\fB\-\-verify\fP] [\fB\-\-quiet\fP]
[\fB\-\-shot\fP \fIFILE.png\fP]
.br
\fBgridsnap\-hotcorners\fP [\fB\-\-conf\fP \fIPATH\fP] [\fB\-\-no\-hud\fP] [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fP]
[\fB\-\-verbose\fP] [\fB\-\-version\fP]
.br
\fBgridsnap\-ctl\fP [\fB\-\-socket\fP \fIPATH\fP] [\fB\-\-window\fP \fIXID\fP] [\fB\-\-monitor\fP
\fIN\fP] {\fBstatus\fP | \fBapply\fP \fIZONE\fP | \fBreload\fP | \fBquit\fP} [\fB\-\-raw\fP]
[\fB\-\-quiet\fP]
.br
\fBsetup\-hotkeys\fP [\fB\-\-list\fP | \fB\-\-remove\fP] [\fB\-\-conf\fP \fIPATH\fP]
.SH DESCRIPTION
GridSnap is a visual editor of work\-area zones and a window tiler for
Cinnamon on X11.  A zone is defined by fractions of the monitor's work area
(panels excluded); a window is placed through muffin's EWMH messages with
window\-frame compensation, so the window frame — not the client area —
matches the zone.  Tiling works on top of the stock window manager and does
not replace window management.
.PP
The package installs seven commands: \fBgridsnap\-tiler\fP (the zone tiler,
\fIzone\-tiler.py\fP), \fBgridsnap\-daemon\fP (auto\-tiling of new windows by rules,
\fIzone\-daemon.py\fP), \fBgridsnap\-editor\fP (the visual zone editor,
\fIzone\-editor.py\fP), \fBgridsnap\-picker\fP (an overlay for picking a zone with
the arrow keys, \fIzone\-picker.py\fP), \fBgridsnap\-hotcorners\fP (zone
highlighting while dragging a window, \fIzone\-hotcorners.py\fP),
\fBgridsnap\-ctl\fP (the client of the daemon's IPC socket, \fIgridsnap\-ctl\fP) and
\fBsetup\-hotkeys\fP (Cinnamon hotkeys, \fIsetup\-hotkeys.py\fP).  All commands are
described on this page; the pages \fBgridsnap\-tiler\fP(1),
\fBgridsnap\-daemon\fP(1), \fBgridsnap\-editor\fP(1), \fBgridsnap\-picker\fP(1),
\fBgridsnap\-hotcorners\fP(1), \fBgridsnap\-ctl\fP(1) and \fBsetup\-hotkeys\fP(1) are
aliases of this one (roff \fB.so\fP).
.PP
X11 and an EWMH\-compatible window manager (Cinnamon/muffin) are required;
under Wayland without XWayland tiling does not work. Hotkeys and the editor
are part of Cinnamon.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS "gridsnap\-tiler — the tiler"
The main command: reads \fIzones.conf\fP and moves windows. The first argument
is the command.
.TP
\fBlist\fP
Prints the monitor's work area (\fB\-\-monitor\fP, 0 by default) and, for every
zone, its pixel frame as \fIx=… y=… WxH\fP; the zones go in alphabetical
order. Does not touch windows.
.TP
\fBapply \fP\fIZONE\fP
Puts a window into a zone: the active one by default, \fB\-\-window\fP \fIXID\fP
sets the window by hand.  The window's maximization and fullscreen state are
cleared, then \fI_NET_MOVERESIZE_WINDOW\fP is sent; the window frame is
compensated via \fI_NET_FRAME_EXTENTS\fP.  \fB\-\-dry\-run\fP only prints the frame,
does not move the window and writes no history.  \fB\-\-verify\fP re\-reads the
actual geometry through \fBxwininfo\fP(1) and appends "matches the zone" or
"does not match!". A successful \fBapply\fP (except with \fB\-\-dry\-run\fP) is
recorded in the history for \fBundo\fP.
.TP
\fBfocus [\fP\fIZONE\fP\fB]\fP
"Only this zone" mode: the active window (\fB\-\-window\fP \fIXID\fP sets the window
by hand) is put into the zone, and the other windows \fIof the same workspace and monitor\fP are minimized (\fIWM_CHANGE_STATE\fP with the value
\fIIconicState\fP). Without a zone the window stays where it is — the mode then
means "only this window". A repeated \fBfocus\fP while the mode is active
leaves it and restores \fIonly those\fP windows that the mode minimized
(\fI_NET_ACTIVE_WINDOW\fP, the target last); windows minimized by hand stay
minimized, and windows under \fB[ignore]\fP and utility types (dialogs, splash)
are neither minimized nor in the way. \fB\-\-off\fP is an explicit exit for
scripts and a second key. \fB\-\-dry\-run\fP prints what would be done without
moving anything. The mode lives on its own workspace and is not lifted by
switching workspaces (restoring is possible only on the active workspace),
and it turns itself off only when the target window closes **while the
daemon is running**; leaving works from any workspace — the command switches
to the mode's workspace itself and restores the windows.
.IP
With \fBgridsnap\-daemon\fP running, the command hands the work over to it over
IPC (if the socket answers): only the daemon catches windows that appeared
after the mode was entered. Without the daemon the mode works once, and the
list of minimized windows is kept in \fI$XDG_STATE_HOME/gridsnap/focus.json\fP.
.TP
\fBkeys\fP
Prints "zone, tab, hotkey" triples from the \fB[keys]\fP section. This is the
source for \fBsetup\-hotkeys\fP. A zone from \fB[keys]\fP that is not in \fB[zones]\fP
is skipped with a message on stderr.
.TP
\fBarrange\fP
Takes the client windows from the EWMH list \fI_NET_CLIENT_LIST\fP and lays
them out into zones.  Only movable windows are moved: a non\-empty
\fIWM_CLASS\fP is required; dialogs, splash and other utility types, as well as
windows under \fB[ignore]\fP, are left alone. The mode is chosen by \fB\-\-by\fP:
.RS
.TP
\fBauto\fP
(the default) first the windows for which a \fB[rules]\fP rule matched, then
the windows without a rule fill the free (not taken by a rule) zones in file
order; extra windows stay where they are.
.TP
\fBrules\fP
Only windows with a matched rule, each into its own zone; the rest are left
alone.
.TP
\fBorder\fP
All movable windows in \fI_NET_CLIENT_LIST\fP order (bottom to top) get zones
in the order of the \fB[zones]\fP section; if there are more windows than
zones, the extras get the last zone and become tabs there.
.RE
.IP
Every window is placed on the monitor it is on. Several windows that land in
one zone of one monitor get a shared tab frame: the zone minus a tab strip
of \fB[tabs] height\fP (22 px by default). The first window of the list is the
bottom tab, the last one the top; a single window in a zone takes exactly
the zone, without the strip. The strip with the titles is drawn by
\fIzone\-tabstrip.py\fP (the daemon starts it); clicking a tab and \fBtabs\-next\fP
switch the windows of the zone. The previous frames are written to
history. \fB\-\-dry\-run\fP prints \fIwindow \-> zone: x=… y=… WxH\fP and moves
nothing.  If there are no suitable windows, the command exits successfully.
.PP
When a daemon is running, the daemon applies the layout: the command decides
which windows go where and writes the \fBundo\fP history, while the daemon
moves the windows, updates its tracker and sends \fIwindow\-moved\fP to
subscribers. With \fB\-\-dry\-run\fP and \fB\-\-verify\fP there is no delegation: those
modes stay local.
.TP
\fBstack \fP\fIZONE\fP
A special case of \fBarrange\fP: collects all movable client windows into one
zone of the monitor (\fB\-\-monitor\fP, 0 by default) as tabs — onto the shared
tab frame, like \fBarrange\fP.  A single window is placed exactly on the zone.
.TP
\fBtabs\-next\fP
Raises the next tab of the active window's zone. The command lays nothing
out itself; it sends a request to \fBgridsnap\-daemon\fP through its IPC socket:
the daemon owns the tabs, it alone knows which windows stand on the shared
frame. The order is bottom to top; from the top tab switching wraps to the
bottom one. A window outside zones and a single window are a quiet no\-op
(code 0), while a daemon that is not running gives a message on stderr and
code 1. Usually bound to the hotkey from the \fB[tabs]\fP section.
.TP
\fBaction \fP\fINAME\fP
Runs the action from the \fB[action:<name>]\fP section — the thing
bound to a key.  The action receives a window and a zone: the window is the
active one (\fB\-\-window\fP \fIXID\fP sets it by hand), and the zone comes from the
record's \fBzone\fP field; without it the zone is the one whose frame (or tab
frame) the window is standing in (outside zones — none). The window of the
subject zone is the active one if it stands there, otherwise the topmost by
\fI_NET_CLIENT_LIST\fP; an empty zone means no window.  The screen is
\fB\-\-monitor\fP, otherwise the window's screen, otherwise the first one; the
grid is the screen profile (\fB[monitors]\fP), then the workspace one
(\fB[workspaces]\fP), then \fIzones.conf\fP, as with \fBapply\fP/\fBstack\fP. A record's
zone that is not in the grid is a config error (code 3).
.IP
What the action receives: the window, the zone, its pixel frame, the
monitor, the workspace, the window's class and title, the config path — as
environment variables \fIGRIDSNAP_*\fP (\fIGRIDSNAP_WINDOW\fP, \fIGRIDSNAP_ZONE\fP,
\fIGRIDSNAP_X\fP, \fIGRIDSNAP_Y\fP, \fIGRIDSNAP_W\fP, \fIGRIDSNAP_H\fP,
\fIGRIDSNAP_MONITOR\fP, \fIGRIDSNAP_WORKSPACE\fP, \fIGRIDSNAP_CLASS\fP,
\fIGRIDSNAP_TITLE\fP, \fIGRIDSNAP_CONF\fP) and as \fI{zone}\fP/\fI{window}\fP/\fI{x}\fP…
substitutions in the command's arguments. The config path in the command is
absolute: the action is started not by the CLI but by a foreign process.
.IP
A custom command (\fBrun\fP without \fIbuiltin:\fP) is parsed by \fBshlex\fP and
started \fBwithout a shell and without waiting\fP (\fIstart_new_session\fP): a key
must not be bound to a terminal that lives until the window closes. If you
need a shell, write \fIsh \-c "…"\fP yourself. Built\-in actions (\fBrun = builtin:<name>\fP):
.RS
.TP
\fBminimize\-others\fP
The same mode as \fBfocus\fP: the window into the record's zone (no \fBzone\fP —
"only this window"), the other windows of the workspace and monitor
minimized, a repeated press restores them.  With a live daemon the daemon
owns the mode.
.TP
\fBshow\-window\fP
Show a window of the zone: raises and restores the window
(\fI_NET_ACTIVE_WINDOW\fP); for a stack, the neighbour on the ring next to the
active one. No zone or an empty zone — a quiet no\-op (code 0) with a message
on stderr.
.TP
\fBmirror\fP
Swaps windows with the mirror zone: the mirror is found by geometry (by the
record's \fBaxis\fP, \fIhorizontal\fP by default — left↔right), not by name. The
window of the subject zone goes into the mirror zone, the mirror's window
comes back; an empty zone reduces the swap to moving one window, both empty
— no\-op. Every zone gets a frame according to the number of windows now in
it (the tab strip), and the previous frames are written to history for
\fBundo\fP. No mirror zone — code 1.
.TP
\fBterminal\fP
A terminal in a zone: the command from the record's \fBcommand\fP field
(\fIx\-terminal\-emulator\fP by default, substitutions work) is started detached,
the new window is looked for in \fI_NET_CLIENT_LIST\fP for up to ~5 seconds and
placed into the zone with a frame for the number of windows plus one. The
zone must be set in the record (otherwise code 1 with a hint); if no window
appeared — code 1 too. The frame is set even when the daemon has already
placed the window by \fB[rules]\fP: the action has the last word.
.RE
.IP
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fP prints the parsed context (window, zone, frame, monitor,
workspace) and the plan: for a built\-in — what it would do, for a command —
the ready \fIargv\fP; nothing is started or moved. Codes: 3 — no such action, a
broken field, an unknown substitution; 1 — a refusal on state (no mirror
zone, the terminal did not appear); 0 — success and an honest no\-op.
.TP
\fBundo\fP
Returns windows to their previous frames from the history: without flags —
the last operation, with \fB\-\-all\fP — the whole history, and for every window
the frame is taken from the earliest operation it appears in. Before
restoring, the window's current \fIWM_CLASS\fP is compared with the recorded
one: if the XID has been reused by another application or the window is
gone, it is skipped (that is not an error).  Only geometry is restored, not
the maximized state; the workspace is remembered, but windows are not moved
between workspaces. The rolled\-back operation is removed from the history
(\fB\-\-all\fP clears the history). An empty history is a success.
.TP
\fBprofile \fP\fIACTION\fP\fB [\fP\fINAME\fP\fB]\fP
Layout profiles: a complete set of zones, rules, exclusions and hotkeys. A
profile is a separate file \fI<templates>/<name>.conf\fP; it
contains only the \fB[zones]\fP section (with the bindings from \fB[keys]\fP),
\fB[rules]\fP and \fB[ignore]\fP, without \fB[grid]\fP and \fB[daemon]\fP. The name is
sanitized by the same rule as zone names; the path cannot leave the profiles
directory. An action takes no more words than described below: an extra
argument is an error (code 2); the profile name is set by \fB\-\-name\fP, and the
second word of \fBexport\fP is a file.
.RS
.TP
\fBlist\fP
Names of saved profiles in alphabetical order.
.TP
\fBshow \fP\fINAME\fP
The contents of the profile file verbatim.
.TP
\fBsave \fP\fINAME\fP
Save the zones of the current \fIzones.conf\fP (with their bindings), its
\fB[rules]\fP and \fB[ignore]\fP as a profile.  An empty set of zones is not saved
(code 2).
.TP
\fBuse \fP\fINAME\fP
Overwrite in the active \fIzones.conf\fP the \fB[zones]\fP, \fB[rules]\fP,
\fB[ignore]\fP and \fB[keys]\fP sections with the profile's contents, keeping
\fB[grid]\fP, \fB[daemon]\fP and the other sections. After writing,
\fBsetup\-hotkeys.py\fP is started with the same \fB\-\-conf\fP so that the new
hotkeys work; \fB\-\-no\-hotkeys\fP turns that off.
.TP
\fBuse\-monitors\fP
Assemble the active \fIzones.conf\fP from the \fB[monitors]\fP bindings: the
\fB[zones]\fP of the first screen plus the \fB[zones:<output>]\fP of the
others, merging \fB[rules]\fP, \fB[ignore]\fP and \fB[keys]\fP.  This is how the
screen grid is applied at session start; \fB\-\-dry\-run\fP prints the config
without writing anything.
.TP
\fBrm \fP\fINAME\fP
Delete the profile.
.TP
\fBexport \fP\fINAME\fP\fB [\fP\fIFILE\fP\fB]\fP
Dump the profile to portable JSON (\fIgridsnap_profile\fP: 1) — as a file or to
stdout.  Without a file — to stdout, if \fB\-\-json\fP is given; both at once is
an error (code 2).
.TP
\fBimport \fP\fIFILE\fP
Load a JSON profile into \fI<templates>/<name>.conf\fP; the
name is taken from the JSON, and \fB\-\-name\fP \fINAME\fP overrides it. The main
format remains \fI.conf\fP: JSON is only a portable exchange between machines.
.TP
\fBgallery\fP
List the ready\-made layouts shipped with the package: id, title, number of
zones and description, one per line; \fB\-\-json\fP prints the same in JSON.
.TP
\fBfrom\-gallery \fP\fIID\fP
Copy layout \fIID\fP into your profile: the name is set by \fB\-\-name\fP \fINAME\fP,
by default the layout's id. If the name is taken it is an error: choose
another name or remove the profile (\fBprofile rm\fP).
.IP
Layouts live in \fI<prefix>/share/gridsnap/gallery\fP (in a checkout —
the \fIgallery/\fP directory next to the code); the \fIGRIDSNAP_GALLERY\fP
variable sets a custom directory.  A layout file is an ordinary \fI.conf\fP
with an extra \fB[gallery]\fP section: \fItitle\fP, \fIdescription\fP and an optional
\fIorder\fP (position in the menu, 100 by default). The gallery directory is
never modified: the layout is copied into the profile and lives its own life
from then on. In the editor the same is done by the “From gallery…” button.
.RE
.SS "gridsnap\-daemon — auto\-tiling of new windows"
An optional long\-lived daemon: it subscribes to \fIPropertyNotify\fP on
\fI_NET_CLIENT_LIST\fP on the root, reads the list through \fBxprop\fP(1) \fB\-root\fP
and brings the layout into line. Windows that appear are placed by the
\fB[rules]\fP rules on the monitor they are on; windows that disappear are
dropped from bookkeeping. Utility window types and everything matched by
\fB[ignore]\fP are left alone. The daemon remembers the frame it placed in
\fI$XDG_STATE_HOME/gridsnap/placed.json\fP: if the user has carried a window
out of a zone, the daemon does not return it there again, even after a
restart.
.PP
With \fB[daemon] reflow = neighbors\fP the freed zone is expanded by a
neighbouring occupied zone within the same monitor, and when a window comes
into it by rule again, the neighbour returns to its original frame. A
minimized window does not disappear from \fI_NET_CLIENT_LIST\fP, so reflow does
not trigger.
.PP
By default the daemon raises an IPC socket (\fB\-\-socket\fP \fIPATH\fP changes the
path, \fB\-\-no\-socket\fP turns IPC off). \fB\-\-verbose\fP prints what the daemon
does.  \fB\-\-once\fP processes one window\-list change and exits; if no event
came, it waits 2 seconds and exits (for smoke checks). Stop with Ctrl\-C
(\fBSIGINT\fP) or \fBgridsnap\-ctl quit\fP.
.PP
The daemon also runs "only this zone" mode (the IPC command \fBfocus\fP): the
window is put into the zone and the other windows of its workspace and
monitor are minimized; windows that appear while the mode is already active
are minimized by the daemon at once. Switching workspaces does not lift the
mode (restoring is possible only on the active workspace); it is lifted by a
repeated command, by the target disappearing and by the daemon terminating;
the un\-restored remainder is written by the daemon to the mode's state file,
from where \fBgridsnap\-tiler focus \-\-off\fP finishes the job.
.SS "gridsnap\-editor — the visual zone editor"
A GTK 3 window with the monitor's work area at natural scale. A zone can be
dragged with the mouse and pulled by an edge or a corner; snapping is to the
screen edges, to neighbouring zones and to the 1/4, 1/3, 1/2, 2/3, 3/4
lines. A double click on empty space creates a zone, \fIDelete\fP deletes it;
there are layout templates (2 columns, 1 + 2, 2 × 2, 3 columns, main +
stack, blank canvas), a name and a hotkey for every zone, a "Profiles" menu
(save, load as a draft, delete), saving to \fIzones.conf\fP, a button to
register the hotkeys and a button to check "put the active window into the
zone".  The minimum zone size is 5 % of the screen; a zone of zero area is
not saved.  \fB\-\-monitor\fP \fIN\fP selects the monitor, \fB\-\-shot\fP \fIFILE.png\fP
saves a snapshot of the interface and exits (for checks).
.SS "gridsnap\-picker — the zone picking overlay"
Shows the zones of the active monitor on top of the work area. The zone
under the cursor and the current selection are highlighted; the arrows move
the selection by the geometry of the zones, \fIEnter\fP puts the active window
into the selected zone (and records the operation in the history), \fIEsc\fP
cancels; a left click places, a right click cancels. An excluded window
(\fB[ignore]\fP or a utility type) is not moved: code 1 and a message. The
overlay grabs the keyboard itself, so the arrows do not go to another
window.
.PP
\fB\-\-select\fP \fIZONE\fP sets the initial selection, \fB\-\-monitor\fP \fIN\fP — the
monitor (the active window's monitor by default, otherwise the one under the
cursor), \fB\-\-window\fP \fIXID\fP — the window to place, \fB\-\-dry\-run\fP only prints
the target frame, \fB\-\-verify\fP checks the frame after placing, \fB\-\-quiet\fP
does not print the line about the placement, \fB\-\-shot\fP \fIFILE.png\fP draws the
overlay to PNG and exits without interaction.
.SS "gridsnap\-hotcorners — zone highlighting while dragging a window"
Dragging a window by its title bar highlights the zone under the cursor, and
releasing drops the window into it. It is a separate process: not needed for
ordinary work, it does not get in the daemon's way, and the user sets up a
hotkey for it themselves (as for \fBgridsnap\-picker\fP).
.PP
X11 does not report “a window is being dragged”, so the watcher listens to
XInput2 raw events on the root window (they arrive even when the device is
grabbed by the window manager) and counts a press in the title bar of the
active window as a drag. While the grid is shown, Cinnamon's “throw to the
edge” (\fIedge\-tiling\fP) is muted so that the window does not jump twice, and
it is restored right after the release — the setting is not left off. If
XInput2 is unavailable, the watcher polls the pointer about 30 times per
second. Details are in \fIdocs/specs/zone\-hotcorners.md\fP.
.PP
\fB\-\-no\-hud\fP turns the highlighting off (the window is still dropped on
release, \fIedge\-tiling\fP is not touched), \fB\-\-dry\-run\fP prints what the
watcher would do without moving anything, \fB\-\-verbose\fP writes every event
(press, HUD shown, \fIedge\-tiling\fP, drop) to stderr.
.SS "gridsnap\-ctl — the daemon's IPC client"
Talks to the UNIX socket of \fBgridsnap\-daemon\fP: one JSON command per
connection, the reply is also a JSON line. Commands:
.TP
\fBstatus\fP
The daemon's state: \fIconf\fP, \fIversion\fP, \fIreflow\fP, \fIprofile\fP (\fInull\fP for
now), \fImonitors\fP (work areas), \fIzones\fP (fractions 0..1), \fIzones_px\fP
(pixel frames for every monitor), \fIwindows\fP (window, zone, monitor) and
\fIfilled\fP (expanded reflow neighbours).
.TP
\fBapply \fP\fIZONE\fP
Put a window into a zone: \fB\-\-window\fP \fIXID\fP (the active window by default),
\fB\-\-monitor\fP \fIN\fP. A success is recorded in the daemon's tracker, so closing
the window then starts reflow as usual.
.TP
\fBfocus [\fP\fIZONE\fP\fB]\fP
"Only this zone" mode: minimize all windows of the same workspace and
monitor except the target (\fB\-\-window\fP \fIXID\fP, the active one by default)
and put it into \fIZONE\fP; without a zone the window is not moved. A repeated
command and \fB\-\-off\fP leave the mode. \fBfocus\fP has no \fB\-\-monitor\fP: the area
is taken from the target window.
.TP
\fBreload\fP
Re\-read \fIzones.conf\fP (grid, rules, reflow); the monitor cache is dropped.
.TP
\fBquit\fP
Ask the daemon to terminate after the current event.
.TP
\fBwatch\fP
Subscribe to the daemon's events and print them: one JSON line per event on
stdout until the connection is closed (\fBCtrl\-C\fP — exit code 0). The
subscription covers all groups (\fIwindow\fP, \fIfocus\fP); there is no option to
choose groups. One line about the subscription goes to stderr (\fB\-\-quiet\fP
suppresses it). No daemon or a rejected command — exit code 1; a zone,
window or monitor in the arguments — exit code 2. Events and their fields:
\fIwindow\-appeared\fP (\fIwindow\fP, \fIzone\fP, \fIworkspace\fP, \fImonitor\fP),
\fIwindow\-closed\fP (\fIwindow\fP, \fIzone\fP), \fIwindow\-moved\fP (\fIwindow\fP, \fIzone\fP,
\fIprevious\fP, \fIworkspace\fP, \fImonitor\fP), \fIfocus\-on\fP (\fIzone\fP, \fIwindow\fP,
\fIminimized\fP), \fIfocus\-off\fP (\fIzone\fP, \fIrestored\fP, \fItotal\fP). \fIwindow\fP is a
hex string, \fIzone\fP is a name or \fInull\fP, \fIworkspace\fP is the EWMH workspace
number, \fImonitor\fP is \fI[x, y, w, h]\fP.
.PP
\fB\-\-socket\fP \fIPATH\fP sets the socket path, \fB\-\-raw\fP prints the reply on one
line, \fB\-\-quiet\fP prints nothing on success.
.SS "setup\-hotkeys — Cinnamon hotkeys"
Reads \fB[keys]\fP through \fBgridsnap\-tiler keys\fP (and \fB[move]\fP — through
\fBkeys \-\-move\fP) and creates Cinnamon custom hotkeys (the
\fIorg.cinnamon.desktop.keybindings\fP schemas) named "zone\-tiler:
<zone>" with the command \fBpython3 <code directory>/zone\-tiler.py apply <zone> \-\-quiet\fP (with \fB\-\-conf\fP
if it is given). The optional \fB[focus]\fP section gives one more hotkey,
"zone\-tiler: focus", for "only this zone" mode; it is read from
\fIzones.conf\fP by the script itself (through
\fBzone_config.load_focus_keys\fP). The optional \fB[tabs]\fP section gives a
"zone\-tiler: tabs\-next" hotkey for "the next window of the zone"
(\fBzone_config.load_tabs_config\fP).  Every \fB[action:<name>]\fP section
with a non\-empty \fBkey\fP gives a "zone\-tiler: action <name>" hotkey
with the command \fBgridsnap\-tiler action <name> \-\-quiet\fP (the script
installs it itself, like the zone keys). All hotkeys installed by earlier
runs (by name prefix) are removed first, so that no garbage is left.
\fB\-\-list\fP only shows the plan, \fB\-\-remove\fP deletes the GridSnap hotkeys and
installs nothing, \fB\-\-conf\fP \fIPATH\fP points at the config.
.SH OPTIONS
Keys of \fBgridsnap\-tiler\fP (common to all its commands unless stated
otherwise):
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fP
Help on the arguments and exit.
.TP
\fB\-\-conf\fP\fI PATH\fP
The configuration file. By default \fI$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/gridsnap/zones.conf\fP,
and if it does not exist — \fIzones.conf\fP next to the code. The flag carries
the profiles directory along with it.
.TP
\fB\-\-monitor\fP\fI N\fP
The monitor number (0 by default). Used by the \fBlist\fP, \fBapply\fP, \fBstack\fP
and \fBaction\fP commands (for an action — the screen on which the zone is
looked up).
.TP
\fB\-\-window\fP\fI XID\fP
The window identifier for \fBapply\fP, \fBfocus\fP and \fBaction\fP: decimal or
\fI0x…\fP. The active window by default.
.TP
\fB\-\-off\fP
\fBfocus\fP: explicitly leave "only this zone" mode (restore what the mode
minimized) instead of entering it.
.TP
\fB\-\-by\fP\fI auto|rules|order\fP
How windows are chosen for \fBarrange\fP (\fIauto\fP by default), see above.
.TP
\fB\-\-all\fP
\fBundo\fP: roll back the whole history instead of the last operation.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-hotkeys\fP
\fBprofile use\fP: do not register hotkeys after activating the profile.
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fP
\fBprofile export\fP: print JSON to stdout instead of writing a file. \fBprofile gallery\fP: the same JSON instead of a table.
.TP
\fB\-\-name\fP\fI NAME\fP
\fBprofile import\fP: the profile name; taken from the JSON by
default. \fBprofile from\-gallery\fP: the name of the profile to copy the layout
into; by default the layout id.
.TP
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fP
Only compute the frame: do not move the window, write no history.
.TP
\fB\-\-verify\fP
Re\-read the real window geometry after the layout and compare it with the
zone.
.TP
\fB\-\-quiet\fP
Do not print success messages (in \fB\-\-help\fP the description is not shown).
.TP
\fB\-\-version\fP
Prints \fIzone\-tiler 0.2.0\fP and exits.
.PP
The keys of the other commands are listed in the COMMANDS section; each of
them also has \fB\-h\fP and \fB\-\-version\fP.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The configuration is a single UTF\-8 INI file, \fIzones.conf\fP. By default
\fI$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/gridsnap/zones.conf\fP is taken (usually
\fI~/.config/gridsnap/zones.conf\fP); if it does not exist — the file
\fIzones.conf\fP next to the code (\fI/usr/lib/gridsnap/zones.conf\fP for a system
package). \fB\-\-conf\fP \fIPATH\fP overrides the choice. Comments are \fI#\fP and \fI;\fP
(including at the end of a line after a space); the separator is only \fI=\fP,
so the key \fIre:<regex>\fP is not split at the colon.
.SS [grid]
Grid lines: \fBcols\fP and \fBrows\fP — increasing lists of fractions separated by
a comma, a space or a semicolon; the last line must be \fI1.0\fP. Needed only
by range zones.
.SS [zones]
Zone name = one of two forms (zone names are lowercased — that is how
ConfigParser works):
.RS
.TP
\fBx, y, w, h\fP
A free rectangle as fractions of the work area: \fI0 <= x < 1\fP, \fI0 <= y < 1\fP, \fIw > 0\fP, \fIh > 0\fP, \fIx+w <= 1\fP, \fIy+h <= 1\fP. This is the canonical form; the editor saves the file in it.
.TP
\fBc0:c1, r0:r1\fP
A range of grid cells: indices of the \fBcols\fP and \fBrows\fP lines (\fI0 <= c0 < c1 <= number of columns\fP). Read for compatibility.
.RE
.IP
At least one zone is required.
.SS [rules]
The section is optional. Auto\-tiling rules: \fIselector = zone name\fP. A
selector is a case\-insensitive glob on \fIWM_CLASS\fP or \fIre:<regex>\fP
— a regular expression (\fIre.search\fP, case\-insensitive) on the
\fIclass|title\fP string. Line order matters: the first match wins. A rule
pointing at a zone that is not in \fB[zones]\fP is a config error; an invalid
regular expression simply never matches. Key case is preserved in this
section.
.SS [ignore]
The section is optional. Exclusion windows: \fIselector = note\fP. The
selectors are the same as in \fB[rules]\fP (a glob on \fIWM_CLASS\fP or
\fIre:<regex>\fP on the \fIclass|title\fP string); the right\-hand side is
a note for a human and for the daemon's log. An excluded window is moved
neither by the daemon, nor by \fBarrange\fP/\fBstack\fP, nor by
\fBapply\fP/\fBmove\fP/\fBundo\fP, nor by the picker, nor by the \fBzone\-hotcorners\fP
drag watcher; it can be moved only by removing the rule.
.SS [keys]
Hotkeys: \fIzone name = Cinnamon hotkey\fP, for example
\fI<Super><Alt>1\fP.
.SS [move]
The section is optional. Moving a window into a neighbouring zone:
\fIdirection = Cinnamon hotkey\fP, the directions are \fIleft\fP, \fIright\fP, \fIup\fP,
\fIdown\fP (the \fBmove\fP command). Without the section the defaults apply; a key
that is not one of the directions is a config error (code 3).
.SS [focus]
The section is optional. \fBkey\fP — the hotkey of "only this zone" mode in
Cinnamon syntax; \fBzone\fP — the zone the window is put into
(optional). Without \fBzone\fP the hotkey only minimizes windows without moving
the target. An unknown key in the section and an empty \fBzone\fP value are a
config error (code 3). The section is carried over by \fBsetup\-hotkeys\fP (the
name "zone\-tiler: focus") and by the editor; it is not part of layout
profiles.
.SS [tabs]
The section is optional. \fBkey\fP — the hotkey for "the next window of the
zone" (the \fBtabs\-next\fP command) in Cinnamon syntax; \fBheight\fP — the tab
strip height in pixels, an integer greater than zero (22 by
default). Without \fBkey\fP no hotkey is registered; \fBheight\fP is read without
it as well.  An unknown key in the section and garbage in \fBheight\fP (not a
number, zero, negative) are a config error (code 3): a strip of zero height
is invisible, and the tabs would silently become invisible.  The section is
carried over by \fBsetup\-hotkeys\fP (the name "zone\-tiler: tabs\-next") and by
the editor — verbatim, as a foreign section; it is not part of layout
profiles.
.SS [action:<name>]
The section is optional: one section is one instance of an action, the name
from \fI[a\-z0\-9_\-]\fP (the \fBaction <name>\fP command). Fields:
.RS
.TP
\fBrun\fP
Required: \fIbuiltin:<name>\fP (\fIminimize\-others\fP, \fIshow\-window\fP,
\fImirror\fP, \fIterminal\fP) or a custom command — it is parsed by \fBshlex\fP,
without a shell and without waiting.
.TP
\fBzone\fP
The subject zone: the window is looked for in it (the active one if it is
there, otherwise the topmost by \fI_NET_CLIENT_LIST\fP). Without the field the
subject is the active window and the zone it stands in.
.TP
\fBkey\fP
The Cinnamon hotkey for \fBsetup\-hotkeys\fP; the hotkey name is "zone\-tiler:
action <name>".
.TP
\fBcommand\fP
Only for \fIbuiltin:terminal\fP: what to start the terminal with
(\fIx\-terminal\-emulator\fP by default).
.TP
\fBaxis\fP
Only for \fIbuiltin:mirror\fP: the mirror axis, \fIhorizontal\fP (the default) or
\fIvertical\fP.
.RE
.IP
An empty field, an unknown field of the section, \fBcommand\fP or \fBaxis\fP not
with its own action, an axis that is not in the list, a section name that is
not from \fI[a\-z0\-9_\-]\fP — a config error (code 3): a typo would leave a
person without an action or without a key. The editor does not edit the
section and carries it over verbatim, like \fB[focus]\fP/\fB[tabs]\fP.
.SS [workspaces]
The section is optional. A grid of its own per Cinnamon workspace:
\fIworkspace number = profile name\fP (EWMH numbering, from zero). The daemon
takes the grid by the window's workspace and does reflow only on the visible
workspace.  Edited in the editor ("Workspace profiles…").
.SS [monitors]
The section is optional. A grid of its own per screen: \fIoutput name = profile name\fP (\fIHDMI\-1\fP, \fIeDP\-1\fP). The screen beats the workspace:
\fBapply\fP/\fBmove\fP/\fBarrange\fP/\fBstack\fP look the zone up first in the screen
profile (\fB[monitors]\fP), then the workspace one (\fB[workspaces]\fP), then in
\fIzones.conf\fP. The snapshot of the bindings is assembled into the active
config by \fBprofile use\-monitors\fP, which is what autostart calls at session
start.
.SS [daemon]
The section is optional. \fBreflow = off | neighbors\fP (\fIoff\fP by default;
case and spaces do not matter, an unknown value is treated as \fIoff\fP). The
\fIneighbors\fP mode is described in the COMMANDS section.
.SS theme.conf
An optional second INI file next to \fIzones.conf\fP: one palette for the
surfaces GridSnap draws itself — the picker overlay, the hot\-corners HUD, the
tab strip and the editor. The section \fB[theme]\fP holds the switches,
\fB[colors]\fP the colours (a colour in \fB[theme]\fP is accepted too). Without
the file every surface keeps its built\-in colours. Unknown keys are ignored; a
malformed value keeps the base colour and prints one warning on stderr, so a
typo never hides the grid.
.PP
Colours are \fI#RRGGBB\fP: \fIbg\fP (behind everything), \fIbg_panel\fP (the tab
strip, the editor canvas), \fIbg_card\fP (a tab, a button, an entry),
\fIbg_hover\fP (the active tab, a hovered button), \fIfg\fP, \fIfg_muted\fP,
\fIaccent\fP (the selection, the active tab stripe, a checked button),
\fIborder\fP, \fIdanger\fP, \fIok\fP, and \fIzones\fP — up to 16 zone colours
separated by commas; without \fIzones\fP eight hues are derived from
\fIaccent\fP.
.PP
In \fB[theme]\fP: \fBpreset\fP is one of \fI43pr\fP, \fInord\fP,
\fItokyo\-night\fP, \fIcatppuccin\-mocha\fP, \fIgraphite\fP (the \fB[colors]\fP
values override whatever the preset names); \fBradius\fP is the zone corner
radius in px, 0..64, \fI10\fP by default; \fBfont\fP is the Pango family of the
labels, \fISans\fP by default.
.PP
The editor's “From the wallpaper…” action writes this file from the desktop
picture and marks its first line as generated; the running daemon then follows
\fIpicture\-uri\fP and rewrites the palette when the image changes. Deleting the
file — or removing that first line — returns the built\-in colours and stops the
refresh.
.PP
The file does not theme window decorations: Muffin never reads a metacity
theme, so the borders of server\-side decorated windows stay as it draws them.
The editor can also write a GTK 3 controls theme with the same palette; picking
it in Cinnamon Settings is the user's action.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
gridsnap\-tiler list
gridsnap\-tiler apply main \-\-dry\-run
gridsnap\-tiler apply bottom \-\-monitor 1 \-\-verify
gridsnap\-tiler arrange \-\-by order
gridsnap\-tiler stack main
gridsnap\-tiler tabs\-next
gridsnap\-tiler undo \-\-all
gridsnap\-tiler focus main
gridsnap\-tiler focus \-\-off
gridsnap\-tiler action mirror
gridsnap\-tiler action term\-main \-\-dry\-run
gridsnap\-tiler profile save work
gridsnap\-tiler profile use work \-\-no\-hotkeys
gridsnap\-tiler profile gallery
gridsnap\-tiler profile from\-gallery work \-\-name mine
gridsnap\-tiler keys
gridsnap\-daemon \-\-verbose
gridsnap\-daemon \-\-once \-\-conf ~/.config/gridsnap/zones.conf
gridsnap\-ctl status
gridsnap\-ctl apply main \-\-monitor 1
gridsnap\-ctl focus main
gridsnap\-ctl focus \-\-off
gridsnap\-ctl watch
setup\-hotkeys \-\-list
gridsnap\-picker \-\-select top\-right
gridsnap\-hotcorners \-\-dry\-run \-\-verbose
gridsnap\-editor \-\-conf ~/.config/gridsnap/zones.conf
.fi
.SH HOTKEYS
Hotkeys are set in the \fB[keys]\fP section of \fIzones.conf\fP and registered in
Cinnamon by the \fBsetup\-hotkeys\fP command (or by a button in the editor) as
custom hotkeys named "zone\-tiler: <zone>" that run \fBgridsnap\-tiler apply <zone> \-\-quiet\fP. After editing \fB[keys]\fP by hand you have to
re\-run \fBsetup\-hotkeys\fP, otherwise the new zone names will not be tied to
keys.
.PP
The shipped \fIzones.conf\fP has four zones and four hotkeys: \fImain\fP —
\fI<Super><Alt>1\fP, \fItop\-right\fP —
\fI<Super><Alt>2\fP, \fItop\-far\fP —
\fI<Super><Alt>3\fP, \fIbottom\fP —
\fI<Super><Alt>4\fP.
.PP
The optional \fB[focus]\fP section gives the key of "only this zone" mode
(\fBgridsnap\-tiler focus\fP): it is registered under the name "zone\-tiler:
focus", so \fBsetup\-hotkeys \-\-remove\fP removes it together with the zone keys.
.PP
The optional \fB[tabs]\fP section gives the "next window of the zone" key
(\fBgridsnap\-tiler tabs\-next\fP) under the name "zone\-tiler: tabs\-next". The
key switches tabs in the zone of the active window; without a running daemon
there is nothing to switch.
.PP
\fB[action:<name>]\fP sections with a non\-empty \fBkey\fP give action keys
under the name "zone\-tiler: action <name>": the command is
\fBgridsnap\-tiler action <name> \-\-quiet\fP, that is, the same path as
the zone keys. \fBsetup\-hotkeys \-\-remove\fP removes them together with the
rest.
.PP
Switching a profile with one key is an ordinary Cinnamon custom hotkey that
calls \fBgridsnap\-tiler profile use <name>\fP: the project does not
create keys of its own for profiles.  A hotkey for \fBgridsnap\-picker\fP (for
example \fISuper+Z\fP) is set up by the user himself — \fBsetup\-hotkeys\fP
installs only the zone keys from \fB[keys]\fP.
.SH FILES
.TP
\fI~/.config/gridsnap/zones.conf\fP
The active config (honouring \fI$XDG_CONFIG_HOME\fP). If the file does not
exist, \fIzones.conf\fP next to the code (\fI/usr/lib/gridsnap/zones.conf\fP) is
read and written.
.TP
\fI~/.config/gridsnap/theme.conf\fP
The optional palette of the GridSnap surfaces, in the directory of the active
\fIzones.conf\fP. The editor writes it from the wallpaper and the daemon keeps
it in step while its first line says it was generated; deleting it returns the
built\-in colours.
.TP
\fI~/.local/state/gridsnap/history.json\fP
The layout history (honouring \fI$XDG_STATE_HOME\fP): JSON, the last 20
operations, written atomically. Needed by \fBundo\fP in a separate process.
.TP
\fI$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/gridsnap/daemon.sock\fP
The daemon's UNIX socket (mode 0600, directory 0700). Without
\fI$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR\fP — \fI/tmp/gridsnap\-<uid>/daemon.sock\fP.
.TP
\fI~/.config/gridsnap/templates/*.conf\fP
Layout profiles (honouring \fI$XDG_CONFIG_HOME\fP; with \fB\-\-conf\fP \fIPATH\fP — the
\fItemplates\fP directory next to that file).
.TP
\fI/usr/share/gridsnap/gallery/*.conf\fP
Ready\-made layouts shipped with the package (in a checkout — \fIgallery/\fP
next to the code). The \fI$GRIDSNAP_GALLERY\fP variable sets a custom
directory. A copy of a layout into a profile is made by \fBprofile from\-gallery\fP; the directory itself is never modified.
.TP
\fI/usr/lib/gridsnap\fP
The package code: \fIzone\-tiler.py\fP, \fIzone\-daemon.py\fP, \fIzone\-editor.py\fP,
\fIzone\-picker.py\fP, \fIzone\-tabstrip.py\fP, \fIgridsnap\-ctl\fP, \fIsetup\-hotkeys.py\fP
and the helper modules, as well as the shipped \fIzones.conf\fP.
.TP
\fI/usr/bin/gridsnap\-tiler\fP
The command launchers (\fIgridsnap\-tiler\fP, \fIgridsnap\-daemon\fP,
\fIgridsnap\-editor\fP, \fIgridsnap\-picker\fP, \fIgridsnap\-ctl\fP, \fIsetup\-hotkeys\fP) —
wrappers around the code in \fI/usr/lib/gridsnap\fP.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
\fB0\fP
Success. \fBarrange\fP and \fBstack\fP with no suitable windows and \fBundo\fP with
an empty history are 0 too.
.TP
\fB1\fP
A runtime error: no display or no active window, \fI_NET_CLIENT_LIST\fP not
read, the IPC socket could not be raised (daemon), no connection to the
daemon or the command was refused (\fBgridsnap\-ctl\fP), a \fBgsettings\fP(1) error
(\fBsetup\-hotkeys\fP).
.TP
\fB2\fP
An argument error: an unknown command or zone, a bad profile name, a wrong
flag.  It is reported by \fBargparse\fP or by the command itself.
.TP
\fB3\fP
A config error: the file is missing, there is no zone at all, broken
numbers, fractions outside 0..1, a rule for an unknown zone, a missing or
broken profile. \fBgridsnap\-tiler\fP and \fBgridsnap\-daemon\fP answer like this;
\fBgridsnap\-picker\fP exits with code 1 in the same cases.
.PP
\fBgridsnap\-ctl\fP has codes 0, 1 and 2; \fBgridsnap\-daemon\fP — 0, 1 (socket) and
3 (config); \fBgridsnap\-editor\fP and \fBgridsnap\-picker\fP — 0 and 1 (plus 2 from
\fBargparse\fP); \fBsetup\-hotkeys\fP — 0, 1 and 2.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
\fBgridsnap\-tiler\fP(1), \fBgridsnap\-daemon\fP(1), \fBgridsnap\-editor\fP(1),
\fBgridsnap\-picker\fP(1), \fBgridsnap\-hotcorners\fP(1), \fBgridsnap\-ctl\fP(1),
\fBsetup\-hotkeys\fP(1), \fBman\fP(1), \fBxprop\fP(1), \fBxwininfo\fP(1),
\fBgsettings\fP(1).
.PP
The package documentation is in \fI/usr/share/doc/gridsnap/\fP (README, SPEC
and \fIdocs/\fP).
